Title:
Automated DeepAgents/LangChain Pipeline for Zero-Days (DeepZero) — Scanning Signed Windows Kernel Drivers

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post describes “DeepZero,” an automated pipeline to triage and analyze thousands of Windows kernel drivers (.sys) to find exploitable vulnerabilities, prioritizing signed drivers that can be loaded without test-signing. It builds heuristics from the LOLDrivers dataset (common dangerous kernel imports + usermode-accessible device/IOCTL surface), then scales analysis across a large driver pack (~12k drivers) with SHA256 deduplication. Candidates are decompiled headlessly with Ghidra, scanned with custom Semgrep rules for vulnerability patterns (e.g., unsafe METHOD_NEITHER handling, user-controlled arguments reaching kernel memory/IO sinks), and only then sent to an LLM (Gemini 2.5 Pro via Vertex AI) to trace IOCTL dataflow and produce VULNERABLE/SAFE reports with evidence. The author manually validates flagged findings with WinDbg and uses an additional LLM to help draft PoCs, resulting in discovery of an ASUS driver zero-day later assigned CVE-2026-13585. It’s useful for kernel vuln researchers, red teams, and driver security auditors who need scalable discovery workflows and cost-controlled automation.

Technical Focus:
- Windows kernel driver IOCTL attack surface discovery (device objects, dispatch tables)
- PE import-table heuristics and LOLDrivers-derived risk scoring
- Headless Ghidra decompilation + scripted dispatch/DriverEntry tracing (Jython)
- Semgrep rule-writing for decompiled C vulnerability patterns
- LLM-assisted dataflow reasoning over decompiler output (LangChain/DeepAgents, Gemini/Vertex AI)
- Cost/throughput optimization (deduplication, candidate filtering, prioritization)

Use Cases:
- Hunting for LPE primitives in signed third-party Windows drivers
- Building a scalable “driver triage → decompile → pattern match → confirm” pipeline
- Rapidly prioritizing drivers for manual reverse engineering and dynamic validation
- Generating structured vulnerability reports to support responsible disclosure
- Creating a repeatable workflow for scanning vendor driver bundles and driver packs

Keywords:
Windows kernel drivers, IOCTL, dispatch handler, DriverEntry, device object, signed drivers, LOLDrivers, PE parsing, import table, pefile, Ghidra headless, Jython, decompilation, Semgrep, METHOD_NEITHER, ProbeForRead, MmMapIoSpace, MmAllocateContiguousMemory, IoAllocateMdl, MmMapLockedPages, __readmsr, __writemsr, LangChain, DeepAgents, Gemini 2.5 Pro, Vertex AI, WinDbg, CVE-2026-13585, ASUS bsitf.sys, arbitrary physical memory mapping